[b]CSS 1.0.0-beta.6[/b] (2026-09-29)

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ngodn/CustomShellSystem/main/docs/media/v1.0.0-beta.6/noncss-row-aemeath.jpg[/img]
[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ngodn/CustomShellSystem/main/docs/media/v1.0.0-beta.6/noncss-row-proxima.jpg[/img]
That's Aemeath's Harbinger mod and ProximaFitShape, two normal replacement mods, worn on Genessa. No CSS package needed.

Hey everyone. Smaller update this time, but it's one I've wanted for a while: CSS now sees the regular replacement mods you already have in your folder and lets you wear them on any shell.

[size=4][b]What's new[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Use Non-CSS Mod.[/b] New row on the Shell tab, right under Use NPC / Enemy. CSS peeks into Content/Paks, finds the mods that swap a shell, an enemy or an NPC (the classic mesh-replacer kind), and lists them by name. Pick one and boom, your current shell wears that look. Abilities and progress stay yours, same as Use NPC / Enemy. Left and Right flip through the looks, the details panel groups them per mod, F opens search. Weapon mods and stuff like that won't show up because they don't touch a character.
[*][b]It knows what's already on.[/b] Wearing Proxima with a Proxima replacer installed? The row will tell you the game is already showing it, nothing to wear. Two mods fighting over the same character? It names both, because the game only keeps one of them anyway.
[*][b]A heads-up, not a block.[/b] Some mods replace stuff that every CSS look leans on: the human skeleton, the player's animation graphs, the shell base blueprints. CSS can't stop those and honestly it shouldn't, it's your mod folder. It just tells you in the row and in CSS.log, so if a look refuses to apply or moves weird, you know which container to pull.
[*][b]Customize and Locomotion tell you what these looks can do.[/b] Ground height on Customize, Default plus the CSS feminine idle and walk on Locomotion. The mod author already picked the colors and parts, so there's nothing for CSS to dye. Misc and profiles work like before, nothing changed there.
[/list]

[size=4][b]Nerd corner[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]A replacement mod's container only names its files by a hash, so CSS ships a little table (catalog/replacement-targets.json, about 150 KB) with every game package under the 51 character folders it can wear from. Spotting a mod means reading a few KB of its .utoc and looking the ids up. No unpacking, no engine calls, nothing per frame. It runs once at startup and when you rescan.
[*]Only patch containers count, the ones ending in _P. That's the engine's own rule for what overrides base content, so CSS never mistakes the game's own paks for mods and doesn't hardcode any file names.
[*]If you save a replacement look over an official shell, it now comes back on its own after a restart once the shell list has loaded.
[/list]

[size=4][b]CSS mods and normal mods, both are fine[/b][/size]
Quick thing I want to say out loud. CSS was never meant to be "the" way to mod Mortal Shell II. I'm not trying to set a standard, I'm trying to push customization further than a plain mesh swap can go: dyes, parts, physics, animations, profiles, all that. Regular replacement mods are great, lots of people make them, lots of people like them, and from this version CSS treats them like family on the Shell tab. Use both. Mix them. Enjoy your mods however you like.

[size=4][b]For modders[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]You don't have to change anything. If your container ends in _P and replaces a shell, enemy or NPC at its original path, CSS picks it up by itself and names it after the file. So give the file a nice name, that's what people will see (ProximaFitShape_P shows up as "ProximaFitShape").
[*]Retextures count too. Anything under a character's folder puts that character's looks on the list.
[*]If your mod also replaces shared stuff (SKEL_Human_Skeleton, the player ABP or blend spaces, the shell base blueprints), players will see a warning with your container's name. Not a block, just info, but good to know.
[*]Want dyes, parts, motion presets or your own animations? That's what a CSS package adds on top. Docs are in the repo: css-packages.md and replacement-mods.md.
[/list]

[size=4][b]When something breaks[/b][/size]
Please, please send the logs with your comment, or PM them to me with a screenshot if you don't want to post them publicly. Think of it like going to the doctor. If you walk in and just say "it hurts", I can't tell you if it's a stomach bug or the thing you ate last night. The logs are the x-ray. Grab these after the problem happens and before you restart the game:
[code]MortalShell2/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/UE4SS.log
MortalShell2/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/Mods/CustomShellSystem/CSS.log
MortalShell2/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/Mods/CustomShellSystem/runtime/status.json[/code]
Your mod list helps a lot too, replacement mods included. Especially now.

[size=4][b]Thank you[/b][/size]
Seriously, thank you. The support, the bug reports, the requests, the patience through all these betas, it's what keeps me going on this. If you want to show some love, an endorse and a vote on the mod page go a long way, and if you feel like chipping in you can buy me a pack of cigarettes on [url=https://ko-fi.com/maplestar86]Ko-fi[/url] or [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/Core/Libs/Common/Widgets/PayPalPopUp?user=168682108]PayPal[/url]. Zero pressure, CSS stays free no matter what.

